The history of the computer stretches from ancient computing tools like the abacus to the development of modern electronic devices, passing through several "generations" defined by major technological shifts. 
Ancient and mechanical aids
Abacus (c. 2700 BCE): One of the earliest known computing devices, the abacus used beads on rods to perform arithmetic calculations.
Antikythera Mechanism (c. 200–60 BCE): This ancient Greek analogue computer used a complex system of bronze gears to calculate astronomical positions and predict eclipses.
Pascaline (1642): Invented by French mathematician Blaise Pascal, this mechanical calculator could perform addition and subtraction.
Difference Engine (1820s): Charles Babbage, often called the "Father of Modern Computing," designed this mechanical device for calculating mathematical tables. He later designed the more ambitious Analytical Engine, which included many features of modern computers like a memory and a programmable "mill" or CPU.
Tabulating Machine (1890): Developed by Herman Hollerith for the U.S. Census, this device used punch cards to tabulate and sort data. Hollerith's company later became IBM. 
The first electronic computers
Atanasoff-Berry Computer (ABC) (1937–1942): At Iowa State College, John Vincent Atanasoff and Clifford Berry built what is often considered the first automatic electronic digital computer. It used a binary system and vacuum tubes to solve linear algebraic equations.
Z3 (1941): German engineer Konrad Zuse built the Z3, the first programmable, fully automatic, digital computer. It used 2,300 relays and was destroyed during WWII.
Colossus (1943): This British computer was secretly developed during WWII to help decipher German ciphers. It used vacuum tubes to reduce the time it took to break codes.
ENIAC (1945): Built at the University of Pennsylvania, the Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer was the first general-purpose electronic computer. It was a massive, 30-ton machine containing over 17,000 vacuum tubes.
UNIVAC I (1951): The UNIVersal Automatic Computer I was the first commercial computer produced in the United States and was used by the U.S. Census Bureau. 
The five generations of computing
The history of modern computing is categorized into five generations, each defined by a major technological advancement. 
First Generation (1940s–1956): Vacuum tubes
Core technology: Used vacuum tubes for circuitry and magnetic drums for memory.
Characteristics: These computers were enormous, unreliable, and consumed massive amounts of electricity, which generated significant heat.
Examples: ENIAC and UNIVAC I. 
Second Generation (1956–1963): Transistors
Core technology: Replaced vacuum tubes with transistors, which were smaller, faster, cheaper, and more energy-efficient.
Characteristics: Computers became more compact, generated less heat, and saw the development of early high-level programming languages like COBOL and FORTRAN.
Examples: IBM 1400 series and UNIVAC 1108. 
Third Generation (1964–1971): Integrated circuits
Core technology: The integrated circuit (IC) miniaturized transistors and placed them on silicon chips, dramatically increasing speed and efficiency.
Characteristics: This generation introduced keyboards and monitors for user interaction, supported operating systems, and enabled computers to run multiple applications at once. They were also smaller and more affordable.
Examples: IBM System/360 and PDP-11. 
Fourth Generation (1971–present): Microprocessors
Core technology: The microprocessor put thousands of ICs onto a single silicon chip, leading to the development of personal computers (PCs).
Characteristics: This era saw the rise of graphical user interfaces (GUIs), the mouse, and networking. What once filled a room could now fit in the palm of a hand.
Examples: IBM PC (1981) and Apple Macintosh (1984). 
Fifth Generation (1980s–present): Artificial intelligence
Core technology: Built on advancements in parallel processing, Ultra Large Scale Integration (ULSI), and Artificial Intelligence (AI).
Characteristics: This generation focuses on developing intelligent machines that can learn and reason, with technologies like voice recognition and expert systems. Current research includes quantum computing.
